## About this package

`${EMGU_LITERT_GPU_WINDOWS_NUGET_ID}` adds Windows x64 GPU inference through
LiteRT's WebGPU accelerator and Dawn's Direct3D 12 backend. It works with desktop
and Windows MAUI applications. It includes five DLLs from the validated
source build: `libLiteRtWebGpuAccelerator.dll`, `webgpu_dawn.dll`,
`dxcompiler.dll`, `dxil.dll`, and `d3dcompiler_47.dll`.

The matching `libLiteRt.dll` is supplied by the exact-version dependency
`${EMGU_LITERT_WINDOWS_NUGET_ID}`. That core includes XNNPACK CPU support and
runs on CPU without this GPU add-on. Both packages' `buildTransitive` targets
copy their DLLs beside the executable for build and publish. Keep those assets
enabled. The usual CPU native output in the repository is not modified.

## Usage

```xml
<PackageReference Include="${EMGU_LITERT_GPU_WINDOWS_NUGET_ID}" Version="${CPACK_PACKAGE_VERSION}${TENSORFLOW_VERSION_SUFFIX}" />
```

Build for `win-x64`. A compatible Direct3D 12 GPU with sufficient memory is
required. The core package brings in `Emgu.LiteRT` and the MSVC runtime;
LiteRT-LM applications also need `Emgu.LiteRT.LM.runtime.windows`.

```csharp
await model.Init(backend: "gpu"); // Emgu.LiteRT.LM.Models
// Or use HwAccelerators.Gpu with Emgu.LiteRT.CompiledModel.
```

The five Windows demo chat models have been validated on an NVIDIA RTX 3070:
Qwen3 0.6B, Qwen3.5 0.8B VL, Qwen3.5 4B, and Gemma 4 E2B/E4B. The Gemma 4 and
Qwen3.5 4B wrappers select 32-bit GPU activations. Set
`EngineSettings.ActivationDataType = ActivationDataType.Float32` for those models
when using the lower-level API. Qwen3.5 model loading can take several minutes.
Vision/audio encoders and token sampling remain on CPU. The first GPU load
prepares caches next to the model; subsequent loads reuse them.

GPU initialization can fail on unsupported hardware; catch that failure and
retry with CPU. The Windows MAUI demo does this automatically and offers
Chat → Settings → **Use the GPU** for all five chat models.

This package contains no LiteRT core, GPU sampler DLL, or LiteRT-LM engine DLL. Source
versions and bundle checksums are recorded in `docs/gpu-build.json`.

Launch the application's executable. When hosting with `dotnet app.dll`, plugin
discovery may require setting the accelerator directory explicitly to the app's
DLL directory, because the native Windows executable is then `dotnet.exe`.
